    "https://svsu.wd503.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Main-Campus/Groundskeeper_JR-1310-1": {
      "title": "Groundskeeper",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nJob Title: Groundskeeper Full- or Part-Time: Full time Exempt or Non-Exempt: No Hourly Rate: 18.20 USD Hourly Department: Campus Facilities-Grounds (PM) Job Description: Days/Shift: This position changes days and hours during the year. November - April = Thursday - Monday 1-9pm April - November = Monday-Friday 7am-3:30pm ***exact dates to be determined JOB TITLE: Groundskeeper                                                 GRADE LEVEL: 4 DEPARTMENT: Campus Facilities                                        FLSA: Non-exempt JOB SUMMARY: To assist in the daily upkeep of University grounds and property including, walks, roadways, equipment; participate in set-ups, and moving furniture and equipment, etc. for campus events and activities; coordinate motor pool activities. ESSENTIAL DUTIES & RESPONSIBILITIES: Maintain University grounds; may include applying fertilizers, herbicides and pesticides; planting; seeding; watering; mowing; trimming; pruning; spraying; removing weeds; lining, marking and laying out athletic field markers; etc. Operate standard grounds equipment, such as tractors, commercial mowers, sprayers, snow removal equipment, back hoe, etc. Inspect, provide preventative measures, and maintain ornamentals, turf, perennials, annuals, other bedding plants, bushes, shrubs and trees on campus. Determine causes and solutions to actual or potential turfgrass, horticultural, pest and other related problems, providing suggestions to the Working Foreman. Perform special grounds operations, may include monitoring and setting irrigation boxes, digging water mains, working on irrigation system, repairing pavement, etc. Maintain required records on chemical usage, plantings, maintenance, and motor pool. Clean, care, and provide preventative maintenance on grounds equipment. Remove debris, litter, and weeds from University grounds, walks, parking lots, and roads. Participate in the removal of snow and ice, may include shoveling, salting, sanding, and plowing. RELATED DUTIES: Perform routine grading and seeding operations. Assist in moving furniture and equipment in and out of facilities for University events. Install snow fences and directional signs. Assist other grounds personnel as required. Other duties as assigned. SUPERVISION RECEIVED: Most tasks are performed independently, or as part of a team.  Assignments are coordinated by the Working Foreman.  Administrative supervision is received from the Manager of Maintenance Operations. Assignments and administrative supervision are received from the Assistant Vice President and/or his designee(s). SUPERVISION EXERCISED: May be responsible for supervising student employees. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: High School diploma or equivalent. State of Michigan Pesticide Applicators License. Possession of a valid Michigan Driver License, in good standing. Three years of direct, job-related experience; nursery, landscaping, golf course maintenance, snow removal, etc. Demonstrated knowledge of horticultural/landscape procedures, practices, and equipment. Ability to compile, maintain, and process grounds/athletics records and reports. Ability to begin and follow through with the task(s) or plan identified in the job description, and to take the first step in performance of those duties and responsibilities.                         Reporting of facilities or grounds maintenance needs which are observed. Ability to effectively communicate with others and maintain good working relations with supervisor, co-workers, and the University community. Ability to work alone or as part of a team and to perform assigned duties within scheduled time limits. Ability to follow and apply general and specific grounds safety procedures. Ability to exercise appropriate judgment based on specific situations. Ability to operate grounds/athletics equipment; may include mowers, spreaders, sprayers, backhoe, tractor, field markers, hand tools, trucks, etc. Ability to perform the essential functions of the position with or without accommodation. Ability to pass job-related physical examination, skill test, and/or State/Federal mandated training, licensing, and/or certification requirements within a reasonable amount of time. WORKING CONDITIONS: Many duties are performed outdoors and subject to environmental changes. Exposure to dust, fumes, pesticides and other grounds/nursery/athletics chemicals. Subject to loud noises caused by standard grounds equipment. The above reflects the general details considered necessary to describe the essential functions of the job, and shall not be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities, or requirements that may be inherent in the job.  It is not intended to limit or in any way modify the right of any supervisor to assign, direct, and control the work of employees under his/her supervision.  The use of a particular expression or illustration describing duties shall not be held to exclude other duties not mentioned that are of similar kind or level of difficulty. About SVSU: Saginaw Valley State University is a comprehensive university with more than 100 programs of study for its roughly 7,000 students. Located on a suburban campus in Michigan’s Great Lakes Bay Region, SVSU is committed to a supportive and empowering environment for students, faculty and staff. For eight consecutive years, SVSU has been recognized as a “Great College to Work For” (2016-2023). SVSU emphasizes undergraduate teaching and learning, and community-based research. SVSU earned the Community Engagement classification from the Carnegie Foundation for the Advancement of Teaching, a distinction achieved by only 7 percent of U.S. colleges and universities. By their senior year, 84 percent of students have engaged with community employers and agencies in internships, field placements or some other component of their academic preparation. SVSU is establishing itself as a leader in STEAM education for the Great Lakes Bay Region, partnering with businesses, foundations and school districts to improve students’ performance in math, science and the arts at the middle school, high school and university levels.   More than 70 percent of SVSU freshmen live on campus in student housing that has been rated No. 1 among public universities in the U.S. (2018-2023). SVSU fields 19 varsity sports at the NCAA Division II level. The average class size is 23 students. For more information, please visit our website ,   http://www.svsu.edu ."
    },
    "https://svsu.wd503.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Main-Campus/Director-of-the-Stevens-Center_JR-1325": {
      "title": "Director of the Stevens Center",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nJob Title: Director of the Stevens Center Full or Part Time: Full time Hours: 40 Exempt: Yes Supervisory Org: College of Business (PM) Job Description: JOB SUMMARY:  The Director is the chief staff leader and working manager of the SCFB, responsible for the Center's performance, financial sustainability, member and sponsor relationships, membership and sponsorship growth, educational programming process, governance support, and day-to-day management. The role combines strategic leadership with hands-on execution in a lean two-person staff model. The Director manages the process for delivering relevant family-business education but is not expected to be the Center's primary subject-matter expert. We may also consider part-time candidates, depending on experience, availability, qualifications, and business needs ESSENTIAL DUTIES & RESPONSIBILITIES: Lead the Center's annual priorities, operating plan, staff work, and day-to-day management in alignment with the Executive Council and SVSU. Maintain focus on SCFB's core mission: member value, education, networking, membership and sponsorship sustainability, and responsible operations. Supervise the Program & Operations Coordinator and personally resolve issues requiring judgment, relationships, or organizational accountability. Serve as principal staff liaison to the Executive Council, its Chair, the Dean, and appropriate SVSU units; prepare agendas, reports, and recommendations and implement committee decisions. Be familiar with and adhere to all SVSU policies, procedures, systems, required trainings, and other university-wide mandates and expectations. Serve as the primary staff relationship manager for member companies and sponsor firms, with regular contact to understand needs, reinforce value, and identify retention risks. Lead member and sponsor retention, recruitment, referrals, prospect cultivation, and relationship-sensitive follow-up. Welcome new members and sponsors and monitor membership and sponsorship results, recommending changes in outreach, benefits, or pricing when warranted. Lead development of the annual educational program calendar with the Program Committee and other stakeholders; gather member input and identify relevant topics and speakers. Evaluate and recommend speakers and program formats, approve or negotiate major program commitments within budget and SVSU procedures, and ensure programs deliver strong educational and networking value. Serve as principal host and relationship lead at major events while the Coordinator manages event operations. Develop and manage the annual budget, revenue forecast, membership and sponsorship revenue, event costs, and overall financial sustainability. Provide regular financial and operating reports to the Executive Council and identify risks or corrective actions early. Use a concise performance dashboard focused on membership, retention, sponsorship, attendance, satisfaction, and budget performance; discontinue or limit activities that do not materially support SCFB priorities. Set direction for major member, sponsor, prospect, and event communications and ensure the website and routine communications remain accurate and useful. Represent SCFB in the regional business community and maintain appropriate relationships with chambers, economic-development organizations, professional associations, and other partners. Perform other duties as assigned. SUPERVISION RECEIVED: General supervision is received from Dean of the Carmona College of Business, with governance oversight by the SCFB Executive Council.   SUPERVISION EXERCISED : Functional supervision is exercised over the Program & Operations Coordinator and temporary/student staff as assigned   MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate discipline. Demonstrated experience in organizational leadership, association/nonprofit management, membership or business development, community relations, university outreach, or a comparable relationship-intensive environment. Demonstrated ability to build relationships with business owners, senior leaders, volunteers, community partners, and institutional stakeholders. Experience managing budgets, projects, priorities, and multiple deadlines. Excellent leadership, communication, judgment, problem-solving, customer-service, and follow-through skills. Ability to work effectively as a hands-on manager in a small-staff environment and to learn CRM, email, registration, survey, and university systems. Ability to work a flexible schedule, including occasional early-morning or evening events. Proficient in Microsoft Office Suite and ability to learn new software quickly. Experience and ability to positively interact with all members of the campus community Ability to perform the essential duties of the position with or without accommodations.   PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Experience leading a membership-based association, nonprofit, chamber, economic-development organization, university outreach unit, professional society, or similar organization. Successful record of member, client, donor, sponsor, or business-development retention and growth. Experience working with volunteer boards or executive committees. Familiarity with the Great Lakes Bay Region/Mid-Michigan business community and family-owned business issues; deep family-business expertise is not required at entry.   WORKING ENVIRONMENT & EMPLOYMENT CONDITIONS:   Duties are generally performed in office, meeting, and event settings; some local travel and schedule flexibility may be required. The work schedule may vary, depending on Center operations demands.  For example, events and meetings often require working outside of SVSU's typical 8 am to 4:30 pm workday. All work associated with this role must be performed within the state of Michigan.   Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and throughout their employment. The university does not provide employment visa sponsorship now or in the future for this job classification.  Since SVSU does not participate in E-Verify, candidates for employment are not eligible for STEM OPT extensions.   The above reflects the general details considered necessary to describe the essential functions of the job. It shall not be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities, or requirements that may be inherent in the job.  It is not intended to limit or in any way modify the right of any supervisor to assign, direct, and control the work of employees under his/her supervision.  The use of a particular expression or illustration describing duties shall not be held to exclude other duties not mentioned that are of similar kind or level of difficulty.     About SVSU: Saginaw Valley State University is a comprehensive university with more than 100 programs of study for its roughly 7,000 students. Located on a suburban campus in Michigan’s Great Lakes Bay Region, SVSU is committed to a supportive and empowering environment for students, faculty and staff. SVSU was recognized as a “Great College to Work For” for 9 consecutive years (2016-2024). SVSU emphasizes undergraduate teaching and learning, and community-based research. SVSU earned the Community Engagement classification from the Carnegie Foundation for the Advancement of Teaching, a distinction achieved by only 7 percent of U.S. colleges and universities. By their senior year, 84 percent of students have engaged with community employers and agencies in internships, field placements or some other component of their academic preparation. SVSU is establishing itself as a leader in STEAM education for the Great Lakes Bay Region, partnering with businesses, foundations and school districts to improve students’ performance in math, science and the arts at the middle school, high school and university levels.  SVSU is constructing the first university research facility on Lake Huron to improve public health and the economy in the Saginaw Bay watershed.   More than 70 percent of SVSU freshmen live on campus in student housing that has been rated No. 1 among public universities in the U.S. (2018-2025). SVSU fields 20 varsity sports at the NCAA Division II level. The average class size is 23 students. For more information, please visit our website ,  http://www.svsu.edu ."
    },
    "https://svsu.wd503.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Main-Campus/Clinical-Professional--Medical-Laboratory-Science_JR-1323": {
      "title": "Clinical Professional, Medical Laboratory Science",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nPart time\n\nJob Title: Clinical Professional, Medical Laboratory Science Full or Part Time: Part time Hours: 29 Exempt: No Supervisory Org: Medical Lab Science (JM-CP) Job Description: Clinical laboratory professional who assists with student laboratory preparing and cleanup, executing student laboratory sessions, providing student feedback during lab, and assisting the adjunct faculty member and program director with additional tasks, as needed. Job Summary: The position includes the facilitation of experiential learning. This person will collaborate with faculty in designing, planning, implementing and/or evaluating laboratory education and lab activities. They will also assist with feedback of assignments to support student achievement of MLS program outcomes.   Essential Duties & Responsibilities: Facilitate student learning through the coordination and delivery of lab-based educational experiences. Collaborate with faculty to design, plan, implement, and evaluate experiential and learning activities that align with the MLS curriculum and program outcomes. Assist students when learning new laboratory procedures, operations, and interpretation exercises. Assist instructors and program director in assessing student psychomotor and affective performance during laboratory activities and laboratory competency assessment   Provide timely and constructive verbal and written feedback to students on course assignments and performance in experiential and simulation activities. Perform other duties as needed Minimum Qualifications : Bachelor of Science degree in Medical Technology, Medical Laboratory Science, or a related science Current MLS(ASCP), MLS(ASCPi), or MLS(AMT) certification. Experience and ability to positively interact with all members of the campus community Ability to perform the essential duties of the position with or without accommodations. Preferred Qualifications: Experience with Microsoft Office 365 and related applications including Teams, OneDrive, SharePoint, and Planner. Working Conditions : Laboratory preparation and cleanup is dependent on clear instructions from adjunct faculty member or program director Laboratory assistance during the laboratory session will be in addition to the adjunct faculty member, so there will always be two instructors present. All work associated with this position must be performed within the state of Michigan. Contract position; Varies greatly,  up to 29 hours per week on an as-needed, inconsistent basis (similar to PRN in the clinical setting); Flexibility required relative to hours and scheduling. Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and throughout their employment. The university does not provide employment visa sponsorship now or in the future for this job classification. The above reflects the general details considered necessary to describe the essential functions of the job. It shall not be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities, or requirements that may be inherent in the job. It is not intended to limit or in any way modify the right of any supervisor to assign, direct, and control the work of employees under his/her supervision. The use of a particular expression or illustration describing duties shall not be held to exclude other duties not mentioned that are of similar kind or level of difficulty.   About SVSU: Saginaw Valley State University is a comprehensive university with more than 100 programs of study for its roughly 7,000 students. Located on a suburban campus in Michigan’s Great Lakes Bay Region, SVSU is committed to a supportive and empowering environment for students, faculty and staff. SVSU was recognized as a “Great College to Work For” for 9 consecutive years (2016-2024). SVSU emphasizes undergraduate teaching and learning, and community-based research. SVSU earned the Community Engagement classification from the Carnegie Foundation for the Advancement of Teaching, a distinction achieved by only 7 percent of U.S. colleges and universities. By their senior year, 84 percent of students have engaged with community employers and agencies in internships, field placements or some other component of their academic preparation. SVSU is establishing itself as a leader in STEAM education for the Great Lakes Bay Region, partnering with businesses, foundations and school districts to improve students’ performance in math, science and the arts at the middle school, high school and university levels.  SVSU is constructing the first university research facility on Lake Huron to improve public health and the economy in the Saginaw Bay watershed.   More than 70 percent of SVSU freshmen live on campus in student housing that has been rated No. 1 among public universities in the U.S. (2018-2025). SVSU fields 20 varsity sports at the NCAA Division II level. The average class size is 23 students. For more information, please visit our website ,  http://www.svsu.edu ."
    },
    "https://svsu.wd503.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Main-Campus/Logistics-Specialist_JR-1298-1": {
      "title": "Logistics Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nPart time\n\nJob Title: Logistics Specialist Full- or Part-Time: Part time Exempt or Non-Exempt: No Hourly Rate: 17.40 USD Hourly Department: Logistics (PM) Job Description:   JOB SUMMARY:   The primary focus of the position is responsibility for the receiving, processing and delivery of goods, central stores orders, and the preparation of outgoing shipments.  Supports mail operations and the graphics center as needed. ESSENTIAL DUTIES & RESPONSIBILITIES: Unload, receive, inspect, scan and provide timely delivery of incoming supplier freight and packages from (USPS, UPS, FedEx, DHL, Amazon, others).  Reject damaged shipments when appropriate. Verify incoming shipments against purchase orders for shortages or discrepancies. Maintain accurate records of goods and equipment received to support the University Procurement and Accounts Payable functions.  Process receipts in Workday as a critical step in the three-way match process.  Collaborate with Purchasing and other departments regarding issues associated with the receipt and shipment of goods. Utilize barcode scanning and package tracking systems to support chain of custody tracking from receipt to final delivery. Provide appropriate prioritization for expedited deliveries. Obtain signatures for deliveries when required. Provide a high level of customer service for the University community. Initiate damage claims and resolve any discrepancies with carriers/suppliers. Investigate and resolve errors, omissions, missing shipments and related issues. Prepare outbound shipments and required documentation, including any return shipments for damaged or incorrect items. Receive, process and deliver orders for Central Stores items.  Monitor inventory and place orders to replenish stock as needed. Provide operational reports and data regarding logistics operations activity to the Graphics Center and Logistics Manager or other members of university administration as requested. Load and unload delivery vehicles using appropriate equipment. Train student employees in departmental procedures and use of technology Maintain a clean, orderly and safe work area. Conduct physical inventory of supplies as required Monitor and maintain proper inventory levels for departmental supplies. Assist with mail operations, graphics center and surplus property operations as requested Performs related duties in Graphics Center, Logistics, or Surplus Property as assigned. SUPERVISION RECEIVED: Direct supervision is received from the Graphics Center and Logistics Manager. SUPERVISION EXERCISED: Coordinate the daily work of student employees. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: High School Diploma or equivalent . Experience in receiving, shipping, delivery, logistics, or warehouse operations is preferred. Valid Michigan driver’s license and ability to meet the requirements of SVSU Vehicle Policy 3.5-1 Ability to use computerized record keeping, package tracking, purchasing and inventory control systems. Demonstrated Personal computer skills using email, word processing and spreadsheet applications (MS Office) and the ability to navigate and access information on computer systems and web resources. Ability to operate forklift and receive forklift certification within 90 days of hire. Possesses strong interpersonal, communication, and organizational skills, plus the ability to exemplify an attitude of cooperation within and outside the department, including the ability to work well within a diverse campus community. Demonstrated ability to always communicate in a professional manner. Familiarity of US Postal, UPS, Federal Express, and other common carrier rules and regulations Ability to maintain confidential information Ability to work efficiently in a fast-paced, high volume work environment where physical exertion is part of the normal work routine. Ability to operate pallet jack, dolly, hand truck and similar equipment. Ability to work alone or as a team member and perform duties efficiently. Ability to follow and apply appropriate safety procedures. Ability to exercise appropriate judgment and make decisions in accordance with established policies, regulations and procedures. Ability to physically lift, carry, push, and/or pull large, heavy, and/or awkward objects. Experience and ability to positively interact with all members of the campus community. Ability to perform the essential duties of the position with or without accommodation.    WORKING CONDITIONS: Duties are generally performed inside with some outdoor work occurring on a regular basis. May include the delivery of hazardous materials. Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and throughout their employment. The university does not provide employment visa sponsorship now or in the future for this job classification.  Since SVSU does not participate in E-Verify, candidates for employment are not eligible for STEM OPT extensions. The above reflects the general details considered necessary to describe the essential functions of the job, and shall not be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities, or requirements that may be inherent in the job.  It is not intended to limit or in any way modify the right of any supervisor to assign, direct, and control the work of employees under his/her supervision.  The use of a particular expression or illustration describing duties shall not be held to exclude other duties not mentioned that are of similar kind or level of difficulty. About SVSU: Saginaw Valley State University is a comprehensive university with more than 100 programs of study for its roughly 7,000 students. Located on a suburban campus in Michigan’s Great Lakes Bay Region, SVSU is committed to a supportive and empowering environment for students, faculty and staff. For eight consecutive years, SVSU has been recognized as a “Great College to Work For” (2016-2023). SVSU emphasizes undergraduate teaching and learning, and community-based research. SVSU earned the Community Engagement classification from the Carnegie Foundation for the Advancement of Teaching, a distinction achieved by only 7 percent of U.S. colleges and universities. By their senior year, 84 percent of students have engaged with community employers and agencies in internships, field placements or some other component of their academic preparation. SVSU is establishing itself as a leader in STEAM education for the Great Lakes Bay Region, partnering with businesses, foundations and school districts to improve students’ performance in math, science and the arts at the middle school, high school and university levels.   More than 70 percent of SVSU freshmen live on campus in student housing that has been rated No. 1 among public universities in the U.S. (2018-2023). SVSU fields 19 varsity sports at the NCAA Division II level. The average class size is 23 students. For more information, please visit our website ,   http://www.svsu.edu ."
    },
    "https://svsu.wd503.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Main-Campus/Adjunct-Faculty--Theater--Costume-and-Make-Up-Design-_JR-1162": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty, Theatre (Costume and Make-Up Design)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15\nPart time\n\nJob Title: Adjunct Faculty, Theatre (Costume and Make-Up Design) Full or Part Time: Part time Hours: 9 Exempt: Yes Supervisory Org: Theatre (JM-AJ) Job Description: JOB SUMMARY: The Department of Theatre at SVSU seeks a part-time instructor for winter semester 2027 to teach a 3-credit junior-level course in Costume and Make-Up Design (THEA 370).  The expectation is for in-person, on-campus teaching and the ability to teach and mentor a broad spectrum of undergraduate students.  The class meets on Mondays and Wednesdays from 1:00 pm - 2:20 pm.  MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Master's degree in Theatre or closely related field Experience in Costume Design and Make-Up Design Experience and ability to positively interact with all members of the campus community. Ability to perform the essential duties of the position with or without accommodations.    PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: College-level teaching experience REQUIRED DOCUMENTS AND MATERIALS: Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter Contact information for three or more references  WORKING CONDITIONS: Duties are generally performed inside and not exposed to adverse conditions. All work associated with this role must be performed within the state of Michigan. Scheduled weekly hours vary depending on class contract load. Adjunct Faculty are hired each semester on an as-needed basis. The above reflects the general details considered necessary to describe the essential functions of the job. It shall not be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities, or requirements that may be inherent in the job.  It is not intended to limit or in any way modify the right of any supervisor to assign, direct, and control the work of employees under his/her supervision.  The use of a particular expression or illustration describing duties shall not be held to exclude other duties not mentioned that are of similar kind or level of difficulty. Applicants must be legally authorized to 