    "https://svsu.wd503.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Main-Campus/PRN-Athletic-Trainer_JR-633": {
      "title": "PRN Athletic Trainer",
      "description": "Posted: 2025-11-10\nPart time\n\nJob Title: PRN Athletic Trainer Full or Part Time: Part time Hours: 25 Exempt: No Supervisory Org: Sports Medicine On-Call (JM-CH) Job Description: PRN ATHLETIC TRAINER: Saginaw Valley State University is seeking a Part-time or Hourly Athletic Trainer to assist in the daily care of NCAA Division II student-athletes. At the direction of the Director of Sports Medicine and under the supervision of team physicians, the qualified candidate will deliver the highest standard of athletic healthcare care to all NCAA student-athletes participating within SVSU’s 20 athletic programs. The PRN Athletic Trainer will be an integral part of the SVSU Sports Medicine Department and will serve with the highest levels of integrity and ethical standards.  JOB SUMMARY: Serves as a certified and licensed athletic trainer for SVSU intercollegiate student-athletes; provides athletic training services on a scheduled or as-needed basis; provides medical care and coverage during home or away competitions or practices; is an active contributor and member of the sports medicine healthcare team.  ESSENTIAL DUTIES & RESPONSIBILITIES: Serves as a Certified Athletic Trainer for SVSU as scheduled or when availability allows.  Works under the direction of the Director of Sports Medicine.  Organizes and manages the care of student-athletes in consultation with SVSU Team Physicians.  Provides medical care, referral, and medical record keeping as assigned.  Communicates with SVSU Staff Athletic Trainers to alert them to new or on-going student-athlete health concerns so that care can be delivered and coaches notified.  Understands and maintains confidentiality of HIPPA and FERPA protected information; handles information appropriately and professionally.  Upholds and works within the Policy and Procedures established for the SVSU Sports Medicine Department.  The Athletic Trainer will work within their scope of practice as outlined by the Athletic Trainers Board of the State of Michigan.  Provides daily evaluation and treatment of orthopedic and non-orthopedic injury conditions.  Documents in electronic injury tracking system daily; including but not limited to treatments, progress notes, diagnostic imaging, and physician referrals.  Assists with daily facility organization, maintenance, cleaning, inventory, and athletic training room coverage.  Communicates with necessary athletic department staff regarding student-athlete injury conditions, limitations, needs, and accommodations.  Perform other duties as assigned. SUPERVISION RECEIVED: General supervision is received from the Director of Sports Medicine.  SUPERVISION EXERCISED : None  MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor's degree in an Athletic Training Program or Entry Level Masters  Certified Athletic Trainer by NATA Board of Certification.  Currently licensed with a state of Michigan License in Athletic Training (AT).  Currently certified with CPR/AED for Professional Rescuers.  Demonstrated ability to communicate effectively and project a positive attitude.  Strong organizational skills.  Ability to maintain a high level of professionalism with student-athletes, athletic department staff, student workers, and staff athletic trainers.   Willingness to travel and work irregular hours, nights, weekends and holidays as necessary.   Proficient in Microsoft Office Suite and ability to learn new software quickly. Experience and ability to positively interact with all members of the campus community Ability to perform the essential duties of the position with or without accommodations. WORKING CONDITIONS: Duties are generally performed inside and not exposed to adverse conditions. All work associated with this role must be performed within the state of Michigan. The above reflects the general details considered necessary to describe the essential functions of the job. It shall not be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities, or requirements that may be inherent in the job.  It is not intended to limit or in any way modify the right of any supervisor to assign, direct, and control the work of employees under his/her supervision.  The use of a particular expression or illustration describing duties shall not be held to exclude other duties not mentioned that are of similar kind or level of difficulty. About SVSU: Saginaw Valley State University is a comprehensive university with more than 100 programs of study for its roughly 7,000 students. Located on a suburban campus in Michigan’s Great Lakes Bay Region, SVSU is committed to a supportive and empowering environment for students, faculty and staff. SVSU was recognized as a “Great College to Work For” for 9 consecutive years (2016-2024). SVSU emphasizes undergraduate teaching and learning, and community-based research. SVSU earned the Community Engagement classification from the Carnegie Foundation for the Advancement of Teaching, a distinction achieved by only 7 percent of U.S. colleges and universities. By their senior year, 84 percent of students have engaged with community employers and agencies in internships, field placements or some other component of their academic preparation. SVSU is establishing itself as a leader in STEAM education for the Great Lakes Bay Region, partnering with businesses, foundations and school districts to improve students’ performance in math, science and the arts at the middle school, high school and university levels.  SVSU is constructing the first university research facility on Lake Huron to improve public health and the economy in the Saginaw Bay watershed.   More than 70 percent of SVSU freshmen live on campus in student housing that has been rated No. 1 among public universities in the U.S. (2018-2025). SVSU fields 20 varsity sports at the NCAA Division II level. The average class size is 23 students. For more information, please visit our website ,  http://www.svsu.edu ."
    },
    "https://svsu.wd503.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Main-Campus/Human-Performance-Lab-Coordinator_JR-1369": {
      "title": "Human Performance Lab Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nPart time\n\nJob Title: Human Performance Lab Coordinator Full or Part Time: Part time Hours: 29 Exempt: No Supervisory Org: College of Health and Human Services (PM) Job Description: JOB SUMMARY: Within an interdisciplinary, cooperative, supportive, and friendly atmosphere, an energetic and enthusiastic person who communicates well with a wide range of people is needed to provide laboratory organization, maintenance, coordination, office duties and other laboratory related tasks. Experience with a wide range of technologies and desire to maintain our laboratories \"on the cutting edge\" of applied technologies is desired. Laboratory classes may include kinesiology/biomechanics, exercise physiology, exercise testing, fitness technology, functional anatomy and other areas as assigned. ESSENTIAL DUTIES & RESPONSIBILITIES: Primary duties will include: Coordinate Laboratory Courses for the Department of Kinesiology. Provide coordinated direct student supervision within the HPL during each of the fall, winter, and spring semesters, including open lab experiences. Organize and maintain the laboratory to meet all the needs of all laboratory activities (i.e. order and maintain all equipment and supplies). Support and coordinate interdisciplinary activities and research among Kinesiology department faculty/staff and others interested in research activities that would utilize the human performance laboratory. Organize and lead community service events within the lab. Continue offering events/activities in the lab that build new relationships with community and campus partners. Enforce safe laboratory practices. Preparing/cleaning-up of the lab and equipment for classes. Maintaining equipment including computer hardware/software, supplies and service contracts. Keeping records, filing, preparing purchase requests. Provide HPL related in-service training to departmental faculty/staff. Operate in accordance with and maintain CLIA Laboratory Certification.  The lab coordinator will perform a variety of tasks in a dynamic environment on an as needed basis to support the on-going functions of the lab and classes. Development of active research and/or scholarly activities/programs and grant seeking will be actively encouraged. Perform other duties as assigned.   SUPERVISION RECEIVED: General supervision is received from the Dean of the College of Health and Human      MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Master's degree in exercise physiology (or similar area). Proficient in Microsoft Office Suite and ability to learn new software quickly. Experience and ability to positively interact with all members of the campus community Ability to perform the essential duties of the position with or without accommodations.   WORKING ENVIRONMENT & EMPLOYMENT CONDITIONS: Duties are generally performed on campus, generally inside, and not exposed to adverse conditions. All work associated with this role must be performed within the state of Michigan Some weeknights and weekends, including intermittent travel, will be required.   Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and throughout their employment. The university does not provide employment visa sponsorship now or in the future for this job classification.  Since SVSU does not participate in E-Verify, candidates for employment are not eligible for STEM OPT extensions.     The above reflects the general details considered necessary to describe the essential functions of the job. It shall not be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities, or requirements that may be inherent in the job.  It is not intended to limit or in any way modify the right of any supervisor to assign, direct, and control the work of employees under his/her supervision.  The use of a particular expression or illustration describing duties shall not be held to exclude other duties not mentioned that are of similar kind or level of difficulty. About SVSU: Saginaw Valley State University is a comprehensive university with more than 100 programs of study for its roughly 7,000 students. Located on a suburban campus in Michigan’s Great Lakes Bay Region, SVSU is committed to a supportive and empowering environment for students, faculty and staff. SVSU has been named a “Great College to Work For” on nine separate occasions. SVSU emphasizes undergraduate teaching and learning, and community-based research. SVSU has twice earned the Community Engagement classification from the Carnegie Foundation for the Advancement of Teaching, a distinction achieved by only 7 percent of U.S. colleges and universities. By their senior year, 84 percent of students have engaged with community employers and agencies in internships, field placements or some other component of their academic preparation. SVSU is establishing itself as a leader in STEAM education for the Great Lakes Bay Region, partnering with businesses, foundations and school districts to improve students’ performance in math, science and the arts at the middle school, high school and university levels.  SVSU is constructing the first university research facility on Lake Huron to improve public health and the economy in the Saginaw Bay watershed.   More than 70 percent of SVSU freshmen live on campus in student housing that is rated No. 2 among all public universities in the U.S. SVSU fields 21 varsity sports at the NCAA Division II level. The average class size is 23 students. For more information, please visit our website, http://www.svsu.edu ."
    },
    "https://svsu.wd503.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Main-Campus/Custodial-Specialist_JR-1316-1": {
      "title": "Custodial Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nJob Title: Custodial Specialist Full- or Part-Time: Full time Exempt or Non-Exempt: No Hourly Rate: 16.90 USD Hourly Department: Campus Facilities - 3rd Shift Custodial (PM) Job Description: Pay Range: $16.90 - $20.10; Scheduled Days: Sunday - Thursday 11 pm - 7 am JOB SUMMARY:  To perform various cleaning, set-up, and tear-down tasks within assigned areas; may include swimming pool, restrooms, locker rooms, hallways, multi-purpose arena, offices, classrooms, special purpose rooms, conference rooms, recital hall, theater; football stadium and its box office, press box, restrooms, concessions and fixed sitting areas. ESSENTIAL DUTIES & RESPONSIBILITIES: Clean assigned areas following established schedules and prescribed methods; may include removing trash, sweeping and/or dust mopping floors, polishing hard surfaces, vacuuming carpets, cleaning windows and mirrors, dusting furniture, equipment, and ledges, wet mopping floors, vacuuming swimming pool, and washing off tabletops, countertops, equipment, and chalkboards. Arrange and set-up furniture, staging, athletic, and/or physical education activity equipment, etc. working from diagrams, written, and/or oral instructions. Tear-down and rearrange rooms/areas following the completion of activities. Lock and unlock building and rooms, close windows, and turn off lights. Sweep and/or remove debris, leaves, snow and ice from steps and walks at building entrances. Perform special projects as assigned; may include shampooing carpets, stripping and waxing floors, washing walls, windows, baseboards, scrubbing multi-purpose arena, weight room floors, and pool deck. Maintain assigned equipment for safety and cleanliness and report malfunctioning equipment. Load, unload, and move supplies. RELATED DUTIES: Observe and report the need for repairs and replacement of equipment, furniture, and fixtures. Move furniture and equipment as needed for cleaning purposes. Assist other custodial specialist and custodial personnel as required. Other duties as assigned. SUPERVISION RECEIVED: Assignments and administrative supervision are received from the Associate Vice President and/or his designee(s). ​ SUPERVISION EXERCISED: May be responsible for student employees assigned to them. ​ MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: High School diploma or equivalent may be considered. Valid Michigan Driver License. One year of custodial experience. Ability to effectively communicate with others and maintain good working relations with supervisors, co-workers, and the University community. Ability to work alone or as member of a team, perform job duties within scheduled time limits, and adapt to flexible and emergency scheduling. Ability to apply and follow custodial specialist safety procedures. Knowledge of the proper use and care of cleaning equipment and solutions/chemicals. Ability to follow clearly defined oral and/or written instructions. Ability to perform the essential functions of the position with or without accommodation. Ability to pass any State/Federal mandated training, licensing, and/or certification requirements, within a reasonable period of time. Must pass job-related physical examination and relevant skill test prior to being appointed to the position. Ability to begin and follow through with the task(s) or plan identified in the job description, and to take the first step in performance of those duties and responsibilities.   Reporting of facilities or grounds maintenance needs which are observed. WORKING CONDITIONS: Most duties are subject to indoor environmental conditions, some duties may be performed outdoors for relatively short periods of time. Exposure to dust and cleaning chemical fumes.  Subject to noise and vibrations caused by automatic cleaning equipment. ​ The above reflects the general details considered necessary to describe the essential functions of the job, and shall not be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities, or requirements that may be inherent in the job.  It is not intended to limit or in any way modify the right of any supervisor to assign, direct, and control the work of employees under his/her supervision.  The use of a particular expression or illustration describing duties shall not be held to exclude other duties not mentioned that are of a similar kind or level of difficulty. About SVSU: Saginaw Valley State University is a comprehensive university with more than 100 programs of study for its roughly 7,000 students. Located on a suburban campus in Michigan’s Great Lakes Bay Region, SVSU is committed to a supportive and empowering environment for students, faculty and staff. SVSU has been named a “Great College to Work For” on nine separate occasions. SVSU emphasizes undergraduate teaching and learning, and community-based research. SVSU has twice earned the Community Engagement classification from the Carnegie Foundation for the Advancement of Teaching, a distinction achieved by only 7 percent of U.S. colleges and universities. By their senior year, 84 percent of students have engaged with community employers and agencies in internships, field placements or some other component of their academic preparation. SVSU is establishing itself as a leader in STEAM education for the Great Lakes Bay Region, partnering with businesses, foundations and school districts to improve students’ performance in math, science and the arts at the middle school, high school and university levels.  SVSU is constructing the first university research facility on Lake Huron to improve public health and the economy in the Saginaw Bay watershed.   More than 70 percent of SVSU freshmen live on campus in student housing that is rated No. 2 among all public universities in the U.S. SVSU fields 21 varsity sports at the NCAA Division II level. The average class size is 23 students. For more information, please visit our website, http://www.svsu.edu ."
    },
    "https://svsu.wd503.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Main-Campus/Project-Coordinator--Rural-Health-Transformation-Social-Work-Programs_JR-1365": {
      "title": "Project Coordinator, Rural Health Transformation Social Work Programs",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nJob Title: Project Coordinator, Rural Health Transformation Social Work Programs Full or Part Time: Full time Hours: 40 Exempt: Yes Supervisory Org: Healthcare Project Management (PM2) Job Description: ABOUT THE POSITION: The Project Coordinator will coordinate and administer two Michigan Department of Health and Human Services (MDHHS) Rural Health Transformation Program awards within SVSU’s Department of Social Work & Youth Services: Rural Practice Scholars (ULMSW-2027) and the BSW to Clinical MSW Stipend Program (BSWMS-2027). Both programs support the development of social work professionals pursuing behavioral health careers in rural Michigan, with recipients completing a five-year rural service commitment after graduation. This full-time position allocates 70% of effort (28 hours per week) to Rural Practice Scholars and 30% (12 hours per week) to the BSW to Clinical MSW Stipend Program. The position is grant-funded through September 30, 2027, with continuation contingent on grant funding. Success in this role requires strong organizational, data management, communication, and coordination skills. JOB SUMMARY:  Coordinate the administration and implementation of two Michigan Department of Health and Human Services (MDHHS) Rural Health Transformation Program awards within the Department of Social Work & Youth Services. These programs support the development of behavioral health professionals serving rural Michigan communities through student stipends, workforce development, and five-year rural service commitments. This is a full-time, grant-funded term position through September 30, 2027, with continuation contingent on grant funding. ESSENTIAL DUTIES & RESPONSIBILITIES: Coordinate recruitment, application review, selection, and onboarding of program participants in accordance with established eligibility requirements and selection criteria. Administer program awards and stipends in coordination with Financial Aid, including recipient agreements, required attestations, and rural practicum placement tracking. Serve as the primary operational contact for participants and liaison with rural healthcare and behavioral health employer partners. Monitor participant progress, licensure, employment outcomes, and required service commitments; maintain accurate records and ensure confidentiality of student information. Collect and organize program evaluation data, administer surveys, and assist with required MDHHS progress, performance, and financial reporting. Assist the Project Director with budget monitoring, grant compliance, and the transition of participant tracking responsibilities to MDHHS at contract close. Hire, schedule, supervise, and review the work of undergraduate and graduate student employees supporting program activities. Travel within Michigan to rural practicum sites, partner agencies, and recruitment events; participate in occasional evening and weekend activities as needed. Perform other duties as assigned.   SUPERVISION RECEIVED: General supervision is received from the Assistant Dean of Human and Health Services.   SUPERVISION EXERCISED : Functional supervision is exercised over undergraduate and graduate student employees supporting the Rural Health Transformation Social Work Programs.   MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor’s degree in social work, human services, public health, or a related field. Experience in program coordination, student services, or grant administration. Demonstrated proficiency with spreadsheets, databases, and survey tools. Ability to manage concurrent deadlines, maintain accurate records, and protect confidential student information. Proficient in Microsoft Office Suite and ability to learn new software quickly. Experience and ability to positively interact with all members of the campus community Ability to perform the essential duties of the position with or without accommodations.   PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Master of Social Work from a CSWE-accredited program, or a master’s degree in public health or a related field. Licensure as a social worker in the State of Michigan. Experience coordinating state- or federally funded workforce development or training programs. Familiarity with rural Michigan communities and behavioral health providers.   WORKING ENVIRONMENT & EMPLOYMENT CONDITIONS: Duties are generally performed on campus, generally inside, and not exposed to adverse conditions. All work associated with this role must be performed within the state of Michigan, except for university-related travel, which may include domestic and international travel.   Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and throughout their employment. The university does not provide employment visa sponsorship now or in the future for this job classification.  Since SVSU does not participate in E-Verify, candidates for employment are not eligible for STEM OPT extensions.     The above reflects the general details considered necessary to describe the essential functions of the job. It shall not be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities, or requirements that may be inherent in the job.  It is not intended to limit or in any way modify the right of any supervisor to assign, direct, and control the work of employees under his/her supervision.  The use of a particular expression or illustration describing duties shall not be held to exclude other duties not mentioned that are of similar kind or level of difficulty. About SVSU: Saginaw Valley State University is a comprehensive university with more than 100 programs of study for its roughly 7,000 students. Located on a suburban campus in Michigan’s Great Lakes Bay Region, SVSU is committed to a supportive and empowering environment for students, faculty and staff. SVSU has been named a “Great College to Work For” on nine separate occasions. SVSU emphasizes undergraduate teaching and learning, and community-based research. SVSU has twice earned the Community Engagement classification from the Carnegie Foundation for the Advancement of Teaching, a distinction achieved by only 7 percent of U.S. colleges and universities. By their senior year, 84 percent of students have engaged with community employers and agencies in internships, field placements or some other component of their academic preparation. SVSU is establishing itself as a leader in STEAM education for the Great Lakes Bay Region, partnering with businesses, foundations and school districts to improve students’ performance in math, science and the arts at the middle school, high school and university levels.  SVSU is constructing the first university research facility on Lake Huron to improve public health and the economy in the Saginaw Bay watershed.   More than 70 percent of SVSU freshmen live on campus in student housing that is rated No. 2 among all public universities in the U.S. SVSU fields 21 varsity sports at the NCAA Division II level. The average class size is 23 students. For more information, please visit our website, http://www.svsu.edu ."
