    "https://svsu.wd503.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Main-Campus/Assistant-Associate-General-Counsel_JR-1352": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate General Counsel",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nFull time\n\nJob Title: Assistant/Associate General Counsel Full or Part Time: Full time Hours: 40 Exempt: Yes Supervisory Org: Legal Affairs (PM) Job Description: JOB SUMMARY:  The Assistant/Association General Counsel reports to the Vice President for Legal Affairs and supports the work of the Office of the General Counsel in contracts administration, policy drafting, legal research and analysis, compliance and investigations oversight and advice, litigation support, and related legal functions.  The Assistant/Associate General Counsel provides a full range of legal support functions on varied and complex duties, tasks, and projects supporting Saginaw Valley State University, and serves as a key resource to University leadership, departments, and the Board of Control. ESSENTIAL DUTIES & RESPONSIBILITIES: The Assistant/Associate General Counsel function includes the following broad responsibilities:   Review, draft, and negotiate contracts and affiliation agreements on behalf of the University. Advise departments on contractual rights, obligations, and risk. Research and interpret laws and regulations applicable to University programs, policies and procedures. Advise leaders on compliance investigations and provide assistance with legal issues. Monitor legislative and regulatory activity to stay appraised of changes and modifications in laws that affect University programs, and work to incorporate changes in policies, regulations, and procedures to ensure continued compliance by the University. Assist the General Counsel in managing litigation and administrative agency proceedings.  Support preparation of legal strategy, coordinate case-related activities, and assist with responses to legal claims and administrative complaints. Prepare correspondence, memoranda, notes, reports, pleadings, subpoenas, affidavits, legal briefs, and other documents necessary to respond to court and administrative agency requirements. Ensure appropriate and timely responses to Michigan Freedom of Information Act requests and support record retention policies and procedures. Monitor emerging legal developments and trends in higher education law and advise University officials regarding how changes in law may affect current and emerging University business, operations, and policy decisions.  Provide legal advice to the Board of Control in the absence of the General Counsel.  Attend Board of Control meetings as needed and assist in preparing legal materials and analysis for Board consideration. Maintain confidentiality regarding sensitive issues and information and exercise discretion in dealing with sensitive or potentially sensitive topics consistent with applicable professional and ethical obligations. Perform additional job-related duties as assigned or as appropriate.   SUPERVISION RECEIVED: General supervision is received from Vice President for Legal Affairs and General Counsel   MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Juris Doctorate degree from an accredited law school. Licensed to practice law in Michigan or admissible in Michigan within six months of hire. Minimum of five (5) years of broad general legal practice in a private, public, or non-profit setting; or a minimum of three (3) years of legal practice with demonstrated, specific experience in a compliance-focused role or in a higher education institution. Experience reviewing, drafting, and negotiating contracts. Demonstrated ability to synthesize complex information into easily digestible formats. Proven ability to effectively communicate legal language and analysis to non-lawyers, including when conveying unpleasant or unwelcome information. Demonstrated legal and ethical stature, maturity, competence, and confidence to operate with credibility at the executive level, as well as a professional history of maintaining objectivity while achieving appropriate outcomes.  Proven ability to work independently, manage numerous projects simultaneously, and meet deadlines. Demonstrated ability to use sound independent judgment and professional integrity. Proven record of providing prompt and effective research, analytical, and writing skills. Demonstrated ability to build and maintain professional collaborative relationships with diverse groups across an organization, as well as stakeholders and community members. Experience and ability to positively interact with all members of the campus community Ability to perform the essential duties of the position with or without accommodation.   PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS TO QUALIFY FOR ASSOICATE GENERAL COUNSEL: Ability to manage, prepare and file employer-based visa petitions and applications for faculty, including advising on immigration matters and coordinating with outside legal counsel as appropriate. Professional, legal experience working in a higher education or government setting or in multiple areas of law relevant to higher education, including but not limited to: Michigan constitutional autonomy; governmental immunity; First Amendment law; civil rights law; employment and labor law; athletics; student affairs; sexual misconduct; technology; intellectual property; accreditation; insurance law; international programs and activities; compliance; construction and real estate law; or research-related legal issues. Extensive experience conducting investigations, litigating cases or engaging in labor relations.   WORKING ENVIRONMENT & EMPLOYMENT CONDITIONS: Duties are generally performed inside and not exposed to adverse conditions. All work associated with this role must be performed within the state of Michigan.   Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and throughout their employment. The university does not provide employment visa sponsorship now or in the future for this job classification.  Since SVSU does not participate in E-Verify, candidates for employment are not eligible for STEM OPT extensions.     The above reflects the general details considered necessary to describe the essential functions of the job. It shall not be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities, or requirements that may be inherent in the job.  It is not intended to limit or in any way modify the right of any supervisor to assign, direct, and control the work of employees under his/her supervision.  The use of a particular expression or illustration describing duties shall not be held to exclude other duties not mentioned that are of similar kind or level of difficulty. About SVSU: Saginaw Valley State University is a comprehensive university with more than 100 programs of study for its roughly 7,000 students. Located on a suburban campus in Michigan’s Great Lakes Bay Region, SVSU is committed to a supportive and empowering environment for students, faculty and staff. SVSU has been named a “Great College to Work For” on nine separate occasions. SVSU emphasizes undergraduate teaching and learning, and community-based research. SVSU has twice earned the Community Engagement classification from the Carnegie Foundation for the Advancement of Teaching, a distinction achieved by only 7 percent of U.S. colleges and universities. By their senior year, 84 percent of students have engaged with community employers and agencies in internships, field placements or some other component of their academic preparation. SVSU is establishing itself as a leader in STEAM education for the Great Lakes Bay Region, partnering with businesses, foundations and school districts to improve students’ performance in math, science and the arts at the middle school, high school and university levels.  SVSU is constructing the first university research facility on Lake Huron to improve public health and the economy in the Saginaw Bay watershed.   More than 70 percent of SVSU freshmen live on campus in student housing that is rated No. 2 among all public universities in the U.S. SVSU fields 21 varsity sports at the NCAA Division II level. The average class size is 23 students. For more information, please visit our website, http://www.svsu.edu ."
    },
    "https://svsu.wd503.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Main-Campus/Assistant-Director-of-Athletic-Communications_JR-1326": {
      "title": "Athletics Communication Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nFull time\n\nJob Title: Athletics Communication Manager Full or Part Time: Full time Hours: 40 Exempt: Yes Supervisory Org: Athletic Communications (PM) Job Description: JOB SUMMARY: The Athletics Communication Manager supports the strategic communication, branding, and media relations efforts for Saginaw Valley State University Athletics. This position assists in the day-to-day operations of the Athletic Communications department, providing comprehensive coverage of NCAA Division II varsity athletic programs through digital media, social platforms, video production, and game-day operations while ensuring compliance with university, conference, and NCAA regulations.   ESSENTIAL DUTIES & RESPONSIBILITIES: Assist in all daily operations of the Athletic Communications department and report directly to the Assistant Athletic Director for Communications. Abides by NCAA Bylaws 10.1 and 11.1 regarding ethical conduct and the responsibility to positively contribute to a campus culture of NCAA rules compliance.   Support department planning, scheduling, and project execution. Assist with the hiring, training, supervision, and evaluation of student employees. Compile and manage student staff schedules for home athletic contests and special events. Perform other duties as assigned by the Assistant Athletic Director for Communications. Assist with the maintenance and content management of the SVSU Athletics website (svsucardinals.com), including: Rosters, schedules, results, statistics, records, and biographies. Assist in the management of all SVSU Athletics social media platforms (X, Facebook, Instagram, YouTube, and others). Develop and distribute creative digital content for assigned athletic programs. Coordinate the scheduling and distribution of GameDay promotional graphics for all home and away contests. Ensure brand consistency, accuracy, and visual quality across all digital platforms. Execute live game-day updates for assigned sports, including real-time scoring updates, graphics, lineups, and multimedia content. Design, edit, and distribute promotional materials for athletic events, including: Game day programs Game notes Digital and print graphics Assist with on-site game-day operations, including statistics, scoreboard integration, and multimedia production. Provide communication support for SVSU-hosted conference and postseason events as assigned. Serve as the primary sport contact for assigned athletic teams, including: Men’s Soccer Women’s Soccer Volleyball Men’s and Women’s Golf Women’s Basketball Men’s and Women’s Swimming & Diving Baseball Flag Football Produce and distribute game recaps, news releases, feature stories, and videos for assigned sports. Lead video production efforts for the Athletic Communications department, including filming, editing, and distributing content across all platforms. Coordinate photography and videography coverage for assigned sports and special events. Coordinate the nomination process for student-athletes for conference, regional, and national awards as assigned. Ensure compliance with all NCAA, conference, and institutional rules related to athletic communications. Maintain accurate statistics, records, and historical documentation for assigned programs. SUPERVISION RECEIVED: Supervision is received from the Assistant Athletic Director for Communications.   SUPERVISION EXERCISED: Supervision is exercised over athletic communications student employees and game-day staff.   MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor’s degree in communications, journalism, public relations, sport management, or a related field. 1–3 years of professional or graduate-level experience in athletic communications, media relations, or a related field. Experience with desktop publishing, design software, and web publishing platforms. Experience updating and maintaining an athletics website (PrestoSports, Sidearm, SID Help). Experience managing social media platforms, including Facebook, X, Instagram, and TikTok. Strong organizational, communication, and interpersonal skills. Ability to maintain confidentiality. Experience and ability to positively interact with all members of the campus community Ability to perform essential job functions with or without accommodation. Willingness to travel and work nights and weekends.   PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Master’s degree in Communications, Public Relations, Journalism, or a related field. Previous experience working in NCAA intercollegiate athletics. Familiarity with statistical software and NCAA guidelines (StatCrew, NCAA Live Stats, Golfstat). Working knowledge of live video streaming platforms (Blueframe, FloSports, Stretchcast, Boxcast). Advanced proficiency in graphic design, video production, and photography. Knowledge of applicable rules and standards of the Great Lakes Intercollegiate Athletic Conference (GLIAC) and NCAA. Strong written, verbal, and visual communication skills. Working knowledge of digital media platforms, social media strategy, and content creation. Ability to manage multiple projects in a fast-paced, deadline-driven environment. Strong organizational and interpersonal skills. Ability to work independently and collaboratively within a team setting. Working knowledge of NCAA Division II athletic communications practices.     WORKING CONDITIONS: Work is performed in a coaching, athletic, competitive indoor/outdoor environment. Weeknights and weekends, including travel, will be required. All work associated with this role must be performed within the State of Michigan, except for approved travel outside of Michigan as required by the competition schedule.   Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and throughout their employment. The university does not provide employment visa sponsorship now or in the future for this job classification.  Since SVSU does not participate in E-Verify, candidates for employment are not eligible for STEM OPT extensions.     The above reflects the general details considered necessary to describe the essential functions of the job. It shall not be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities, or requirements that may be inherent in the job.  It is not intended to limit or in any way modify the right of any supervisor to assign, direct, and control the work of employees under his/her supervision.  The use of a particular expression or illustration describing duties shall not be held to exclude other duties not mentioned that are of similar kind or level of difficulty. About SVSU: Saginaw Valley State University is a comprehensive university with more than 100 programs of study for its roughly 7,000 students. Located on a suburban campus in Michigan’s Great Lakes Bay Region, SVSU is committed to a supportive and empowering environment for students, faculty and staff. SVSU has been named a “Great College to Work For” on nine separate occasions. SVSU emphasizes undergraduate teaching and learning, and community-based research. SVSU has twice earned the Community Engagement classification from the Carnegie Foundation for the Advancement of Teaching, a distinction achieved by only 7 percent of U.S. colleges and universities. By their senior year, 84 percent of students have engaged with community employers and agencies in internships, field placements or some other component of their academic preparation. SVSU is establishing itself as a leader in STEAM education for the Great Lakes Bay Region, partnering with businesses, foundations and school districts to improve students’ performance in math, science and the arts at the middle school, high school and university levels.  SVSU is constructing the first university research facility on Lake Huron to improve public health and the economy in the Saginaw Bay watershed.   More than 70 percent of SVSU freshmen live on campus in student housing that is rated No. 2 among all public universities in the U.S. SVSU fields 21 varsity sports at the NCAA Division II level. The average class size is 23 students. For more information, please visit our website, http://www.svsu.edu ."
    },
    "https://svsu.wd503.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Main-Campus/Administrative-Secretary_JR-1348-1": {
      "title": "Administrative Secretary",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nPart time\n\nJob Title: Administrative Secretary Full- or Part-Time: Part time Exempt or Non-Exempt: No Hourly Rate: 21.10 USD Hourly Department: Alumni Relations (PM) Job Description: JOB SUMMARY: The Administrative Secretary will provide administrative and clerical support to the Executive Director of Alumni Relations.  The Administrative Secretary will be the “first point of contact” in the department from a personal reception and telephone reception perspective.   ESSENTIAL DUTIES & RESPONSIBILITIES: Provide a range of administrative support (i.e., scheduling rooms/facilities, tracking RSVPs, ordering supplies/apparel/giveaways) for office programming and attend signature alumni events, i.e., Commencement, Senior Toast, Homecoming, and others. Update the alumni database in Blackbaud Raiser’s Edge and respond to requests for inquiries. Verify users and process data in the SVSU Connect platform. Monitor department email accounts and respond accordingly to requests for information, etc. Provide information, print alumni membership cards, and distribute spirit items to alumni visitors. Assist with processing and notification of Legacy Scholarship recipients and generate reports of legacy student numbers for alumni office staff. Process voucher refunds and order forms for Cardinal License Plates. Coordinate telephone communications that originate through the department’s main contact numbers at the reception desk; respond to voicemails that are left on these phone lines. Provide clerical support for the Alumni Board of Directors. Supervise general office student employees, including reviewing, recording, and approving student hours on Ultratime. Coordinate orientation program for new student employees. Organize storage, supplies, and student work rooms. Other duties, as assigned.   SUPERVISION RECEIVED: Direct supervision received from the Executive Director of Alumni Relations. Additional work assignments, direction, and guidance may be provided by the administrative staff.   SUPERVISION EXERCISED: Assist with the training and supervision of general office student employees.   MINIMUM EXPERIENCE AND QUALIFICATIONS: High School Diploma or equivalent, and additional education, training, or certification in a related field. Three years of job-related clerical experience, preferably in higher education, including excellent spelling, grammar, punctuation, and proofreading skills basic math skills, and oral and written communication skills. Knowledge of basic bookkeeping or accounting principles. Personal computer proficiency, including Excel, Word, database programs, Office 365, and others. The ability to learn specialized software programs, including web development. Ability to work cooperatively and communicate professionally with faculty, staff, students, and the general public. Experience and ability to positively interact with allmembers of the campus community. Ability to work independently with minimal supervision and accept direction from multiple and diverse staff/faculty members. Ability to organize, prioritize, and expedite workload, working under occasional time pressure. Ability to occasionally handle differing, non-routine work situations and problems by exercising independent judgment and initiative. Ability to maintain confidentiality of files and records. Ability to learn technical/discipline-specific terminology. Ability to perform the essential duties of the position with or without accommodation.     WORKING CONDITIONS: Duties are generally performed on-campus, inside and not exposed to adverse conditions.  May include evenings and weekends.   Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and throughout their employment. The university does not provide employment visa sponsorship now or in the future for this job classification.  Since SVSU does not participate in E-Verify, candidates for employment are not eligible for STEM OPT extensions. The above reflects the general details considered necessary to describe the essential functions of the job, and shall not be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities, or requirements that may be inherent in the job. It is not intended to limit or in any way modify the right of any supervisor to assign, direct, and control the work of employees under his/her supervision. The use of a particular expression or illustration describing duties shall not be held to exclude other duties not mentioned that are of similar kind or level of difficulty. 2026100901 About SVSU: Saginaw Valley State University is a comprehensive university with more than 100 programs of study for its roughly 7,000 students. Located on a suburban campus in Michigan’s Great Lakes Bay Region, SVSU is committed to a supportive and empowering environment for students, faculty and staff. SVSU has been named a “Great College to Work For” on nine separate occasions. SVSU emphasizes undergraduate teaching and learning, and community-based research. SVSU has twice earned the Community Engagement classification from the Carnegie Foundation for the Advancement of Teaching, a distinction achieved by only 7 percent of U.S. colleges and universities. By their senior year, 84 percent of students have engaged with community employers and agencies in internships, field placements or some other component of their academic preparation. SVSU is establishing itself as a leader in STEAM education for the Great Lakes Bay Region, partnering with businesses, foundations and school districts to improve students’ performance in math, science and the arts at the middle school, high school and university levels.  SVSU is constructing the first university research facility on Lake Huron to improve public health and the economy in the Saginaw Bay watershed.   More than 70 percent of SVSU freshmen live on campus in student housing that is rated No. 2 among all public universities in the U.S. SVSU fields 21 varsity sports at the NCAA Division II level. The average class size is 23 students. For more information, please visit our website, http://www.svsu.edu ."
    }
